  of the collection was from every  corner of the globe this piece as you  can see it was labeled by mr. Miller  the problem carpenter says was that the  FBI found a lot of it had been illegally  obtained Miller admitted he'd gone on  digging expeditions in foreign countries  and around the United States for decades  in violation of Antiquities laws did he  understand that he had obtained some  things illegally he did so he admitted  that he did  Miller eventually agreed to let the FBI  seized some 5000 artifacts so they could  be returned to their countries of origin  but carpenter says all the FBI's careful  planning couldn't prepare them for  another more disturbing discovery about  2,000 human bones mm to the best of our  knowledge right now those 2,000 bones  represent about 500 human beings those  human remains he says were also dug up  nearly all of them from ancient Native  American burial sites okay that sounds  like a staggering number  it's very staggering why would anybody  have that many human bones I don't know  I truly don't know Native American  burial sites dating back thousands of  years have been a source of fascination  for archeologists for decades meticulous  care must be exercised this old  government film showed the excavation of  an ancient Native American village in  Alabama over time many other sites have  been looted by people seeking artifacts  and even skeletons this comes down to a  basic human right  Holly Cusack McVeigh is a professor of  anthropology brought in by the FBI on  the Miller case we have to think about  the context of who has been the target  of grave robbing for centuries whose  ancestors have been collected for hobby  and this comes down to racism they  aren't digging white graves experts  determine the remains found at Miller's  residence likely came from Native  American tribes including the Arikara in  North Dakota tribal official P  Coffee is working with the FBI to bring  them home all too often here we have  been